  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nMainly cloudy skies will persist through the remainder of today\nwith an increasing chance for a few scattered showers,\nespecially across western ME. Temperatures will remain nearly\nsteady into the lower to middle 70s in most locations.  \n\nLatest forecast guidance and upstream observations continue to\nindicate that marine fog/stratus will push inland overnight,\nespecially across western ME south of the foothills as well as\nthe NH Seacoast. It will otherwise remain cloudy with a few\nscattered showers possible at times with overnight low\ntemperatures into the 50s to near 60 degrees in most locations.\n\nClosed low pressure will trek across southern Quebec on Sunday,\nsending a series of fronts across New England. Our region will \nbe within a weak warm sector with partial clearing allowing high\ntemperatures to warm into the middle to upper 70s with dew \npoints into the upper 60s to near 70 degrees. A negatively \ntilted H5 trof axis will arrive during the afternoon into the \nearly evening, acting as a catalyst for stronger flow aloft and \nupward vertical motion. Latest CAMs continue to indicate that \nMU/MLCAPE will increase into the 500-1000 J/KG range with 0-6km \nbulk shear of generally 35-40 kts. Cool temperatures aloft will \nsupport marginally conducive mid-lvl lapse rates, thus \nsupporting the potential for a few strong to marginally severe \nthunderstorms. The primary threat will be strong to locally \ndamaging winds but hail cannot be ruled out within an stronger \nupdrafts. The greatest threat window looks to be between 2-8PM \nwith eastern portions of the CWA the least at play given SE sfc \nflow. \n\nSunday night will feature a continued chance for a few widely\nscattered showers along with fog as overnight low temperatures \nfall to within a few degrees either side of 60 in most \nlocations.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA closed upper low will continue to meander from southwest to \nnortheast Quebec Monday through Tuesday night. This feature will \nlead to plenty of clouds to start the work week as we remain in \npersistent cyclonic flow aloft. While widespread significant \nrainfall is not expected, there should be some isolated to scattered \nshowers (hit or miss) at times and a locally heavy downpour is \npossible. There may also be enough breaks in the clouds for at least \nsome instability to develop Monday and Tuesday afternoons so a few \nthunderstorms are possible. Shear would be sufficient for a few \nstronger storms if updrafts can become strong enough. Gusty winds \nwould be the primary threat. \n\nThe cutoff low then kicks out in the Wednesday/Thursday timeframe as \nupper level ridging approaches from the west, promoting dry weather \nand warming temperatures. The ridge then passes overhead quickly and \nthe next system approaches late in the work week and possibly into \nthe weekend.",
